WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.840
Hoboken, like many other cities, was designed 
in terms of its streets and roads for cars.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.300
And we're trying to change that.

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:26.818
Right across the Hudson River from Manhattan,
the New Jersey city of Hoboken

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:29.863
has become an unexpected leader in pedestrian safety.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:33.491
Just over a square mile big, the small urban enclave 
has made it six years

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:37.954
and counting without a single traffic fatality,
making it an outlier in the US,

00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:41.249
where around 40,000 people die on roads each year.

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:44.502
Part of the transformation
has been led by Mayor Ravi Bhalla,

00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:46.337
who took office in 2018.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.302
I've been a resident of Hoboken
for about 23 years.

00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:55.221
When I was younger,
I noticed carrying a stroller

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.807
with my young daughter that I couldn't even pass

00:00:57.807 --> 00:01:02.395
main street without pushing the stroller 
into the middle of the road

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.106
because we simply had no pedestrian crossing signals.

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:07.442
So I didn't know when the light
would turn in order

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:09.611
to cross safely with my child.

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:12.989
I just thought intuitively
that that was a major problem.

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:16.076
So that's when I started thinking about pedestrian safety

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:20.497
and how we can do better for especially 
our most vulnerable population.

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:24.459
So when I was elected, we started to 
hone in on developing a plan,

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:28.088
which became the Vision Zero Plan,
where we seek to eliminate

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.632
not only traffic-related deaths,

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:34.010
but also traffic-related injuries by 2030.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.599
Cities around the world have adopted similarly ambitious 
Vision Zero goals, with mixed progress.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:43.686
Hoboken's neighbor, New York City, 
signed on to Vision Zero in 2014,

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:48.108
but has seen more than 200 people die 
in each of the following eight years.

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:50.360
Hoboken had a slightly more targeted task.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:56.032
Between 2014 and 2018, the city saw 
nearly 4,500 crashes on its streets,

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.826
three of which caused deaths.

00:01:57.826 --> 00:02:02.038
We set up a Vision Zero task force to engage

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:06.584
stakeholders, residents, engineers, public safety

00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:11.506
to all come together and identify solutions that are innovative.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:14.676
We kind of take a bird's eye view and see what

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:19.097
local-cost, high-impact measures we can implement

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:22.642
in order to make that part of Hoboken a little bit safer.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.645
Hoboken's redesign incorporates lots of examples

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:29.732
of something called daylighting,
which creates more visibility at intersections

00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:32.861
by keeping cars from parking too close to the crosswalk.

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:37.824
So just with a bucket of paint, you can 
actually create a curb extension.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.535
You can create high-visibility crosswalks.

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:46.249
You can do a low cost means by putting in a bike rack or bollards.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.461
We've implemented a program called “Twenty is Plenty”

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:54.799
where the speed limit citywide is 20 miles an hour.

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:58.219
We want to make it easy and safe for you to walk

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:00.013
or if you want to ride a bike.

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:03.183
One of the means we've implemented is partnering

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:07.812
with Citi Bike and New York City 
and Jersey City to implement

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:14.402
a regional holistic program to easily access 
a bicycle if you don't own one.

00:03:14.402 --> 00:03:18.364
And also joining that
with our program for bike lanes

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:20.575
so that you can not only access a bike,

00:03:20.575 --> 00:03:23.995
but you can get from one place
to another in a safe manner.

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:27.582
Hoboken has taken a kitchen sink approach,
pairing street redesigns

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:30.960
with speed limit changes
and a spree of bike friendly building.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.048
In places like Resiliency Park,
you can see the changes in action.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:41.596
So this intersection here kind of exemplifies
a lot of what we're trying to do.

00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:45.099
In terms of daylighting, on both sides of me
you have rain gardens

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:48.228
which serve a dual purpose
of mitigating flooding

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:50.521
as well as providing pedestrian safety.

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:53.149
We have ADA-accessible, high-visibility crosswalks.

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:57.111
Endurablend in the center to alert drivers

00:03:57.111 --> 00:04:00.615
that this is a highly-trafficked intersection,

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:02.492
as well as other forms of daylighting,

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:07.580
such as bollards on the left side, which come at a low cost

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.583
but also a high impact in terms of making
sure cars are not parked there

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.418
and people can walk and bike safely.

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.504
Some roads are a little bit narrow

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:17.715
to allow both vehicular traffic as well as biking.

00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:20.426
So for those roads we create shared spaces.

00:04:21.469 --> 00:04:24.305
Not every road in Hoboken feels safe to share yet.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:29.394
Biking down Hoboken's busy commercial main street 
still means weaving around double-parked cars

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:33.690
because efforts to build a protected bike lane 
have met resistance from local businesses.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.066
Change is not easy.

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:38.152
We did face a number of roadblocks, so to speak.

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:42.490
This is change that is not happening within a day or a week.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:46.577
It's very incremental, but at the same time 
it's also very impactful.

00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:50.164
It's very important for public officials,
no matter who you are

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:53.084
and really the community, to dream big.

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:55.253
It's not expensive to create a bike lane.

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:57.213
This is more a challenge

00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:01.009
of changing the culture of mobility in Hoboken.

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:06.014
If we can change that culture, change that mentality, 
behaviors of residents,

00:05:06.014 --> 00:05:10.184
that will outlast any changes in administrations

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:13.604
and governments because progress, it might be implemented

00:05:13.604 --> 00:05:17.150
at the governmental level
but it really comes from the residents.

